              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The term
            %em stack
            is a traditional synonym for
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-sheaf"} 2-sheaf
            (typically specifically on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            rather than a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-sheaf"} (2,1)-sheaf
            (see there for more details).
          %p
            This is part of a whole hierarchy of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher categorical
            generalizations of the notion of a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-sheaf"} (2,1)-sheaf
            \/ stack is equivalently a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-truncated"} 1-truncated
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sheaf"} (∞,1)-sheaf
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stack"} ∞-stack
          %p
            Generally, the term
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-stack"} n-stack
            is a synonym for
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-sheaf"} (n+1)-sheaf
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2C1%29-sheaf"} (n+1,1)-sheaf
          %p
            More concretely, this means that a 1-stack on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            (or more generally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-site"} (2,1)-site
            or even
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C2%29-site"} (2,2)-site
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudofunctor"}> pseudo
                \-)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
                from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{op}
                to the 2-category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
                of categories
            %li
              %p
                that satisfies
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
                for all covers.
          %p
            If the pseudofunctor takes values in the 2-subcategory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grpd"} Grpd
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \subseteq Cat
            of groupoids, the stack is sometimes referred to as a
            = succeed "." do
              %em stack of groupoids
          %p
            In some circles, the notion of a stack as a generalized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            is almost more familiar than the notion of a sheaf as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+and+quantity"} generalized space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+stacks"} differentiable stacks
            have attracted much attention in the study of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoids"} Lie groupoids
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifolds"} orbifolds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+space"} diffeological spaces
            are only beginning to be investigated more in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+theory"} Lie theory
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "https://mathoverflow.net/questions/93948/link-between-internal-groupoids-and-stacks-on-a-topos"} this MO post
          %p
            An
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+stack"} algebraic stack
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+stack"} differentiable stack
          %h2#provisional_discussion Provisional discussion
          %p
            The following is “provisional” material on stacks that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Todd+Trimble"} Todd Trimble
            wrote in the course of a discussion with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs
          %hr/
          %p (Todd speaking.) I don’t really speak “stacks”, but in an effort to build a bridge between sheaves and stacks, I’ll write down what I thought I understood, and ask someone such as Urs to come in and check. (Warning: I’m treating this edit box almost as a sandbox, in that what I say below is all a bit provisional until we get some discussion going.)
          .query
            %p
              Hi Todd, thanks for this. I started making some remarks on the relation between descent
              = succeed "-categories" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covers"} covers
              regarded as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sieves"} sieves
              (hence as presheaves) at
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent+and+codescent"} descent and codescent
              in the section titled
              = succeed "." do
                %em Descent in terms of pseudo-functors
          %p
            At the simplest level, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            be a category. As we know, a presheaf on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is just a functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X: C^{op} \to Set
          %p
            Now let’s categorify just once: regard a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            as a bicategory whose local hom-categories are discrete. What I’ll call a “pre-stack” is then a homomorphism of bicategories
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X: C^{op} \to Cat
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(f)X(g) \to X(f g)
          %p
            Now suppose that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            also comes equipped with a topology
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            be a
            = succeed "-covering" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi F
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: F \hookrightarrow \hom(-, c)
            %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2007/05/on_the_bar_construction.html"} on the bar construction
            [perhaps this may go partway to addressing your most recent query there, Urs].
          %p
            Namely, there is a canonical way of presenting
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            as a colimit of representables. Officially, it’s given by a coend formula, but it’s probably more illuminating to think of it in terms of tensor products over
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi hom
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi C
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom_C(-, -) \otimes_C F(-) \cong F(-)
          %p
            In the long-winded version, this says that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is the coequalizer of a diagram having the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ,
                      %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi hom
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi hom
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo →
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi c
                  %msub
                    %mi hom
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{c, d} \hom_C(-, c) \times \hom_C(c, d) \times F(d) \stackrel{\to}{\to} \sum_c \hom_C(-, c) \times F(c) \to F(-)
          %p
            where the more visible one of the two parallel arrows involves the contravariant action of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            on
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(c, d) \times F(d) \to F(c)
          %p
            and the less visible one uses
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            acting on itself:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(-, c) \times \hom(c, d) \times F(d) \to hom(-, d) \times F(d)
          %p
            The point now is that this coequalizer diagram represents the tail end of a simplicial object (with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(-)
            appearing in dimension -1), which in the notation of the bar construction one could call
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(C, C, F)
          %p
            The point is that any category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            can be regarded as a monad in the bicategory of spans. The underlying span is of course
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mi dom
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi cod
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_0 \stackrel{dom}{\leftarrow} C_1 \stackrel{cod}{\to} C_0
          %p
            and a presheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ←
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_0 \leftarrow F \to 1
          %p
            and is precisely an algebra over the monad
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            In fact, if memory serves the category of descent data can be efficiently expressed in bicategorical language as follows. The covering sieve
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            becomes a homomorphism of bicategories by changing base from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            to
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                  %mi Set
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi discrete
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op} \stackrel{F}{\to} Set \stackrel{discrete}{\to} Cat
          %p
            and, abbreviating
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi discrete
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} discrete
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Desc
                    %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Nat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Desc_F(X) \simeq Nat(d F, X)
          %p where the thing on the right side is the category of strong (i.e., pseudo) natural transformations between the indicated bicategory homomorphisms.
          %p
            In that case, the stack condition on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            becomes the statement that the canonical functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi Yoneda
                  %mi Nat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Nat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(c) \stackrel{Yoneda}{\cong} Nat(d \hom(-, c), X) \to Nat(d F, X)
          %p
            (where the first equivalence comes from the bicategorical Yoneda lemma, and the second functor is induced from the subfunctor
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi F
                    %mo →
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: F \to \hom(-, c)
            = succeed "-covering" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
          %h3#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              The stack of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi BG
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} BG
              is a functor
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Mfd
                        %mi op
                      %mo →
                      %mi Gpd
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mfd^{op} \to Gpd
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Mfd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\in Mfd
              to the groupoid of
              = succeed "-principal" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              with
              = succeed "-equivariant" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi f
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\xrightarrow{f} V
              to the functor induced by pullbacks of principal bundles via
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi BG
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} BG
              comes from the prestack
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi BG
                      %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi Mfd
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Gpd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} BG^p: Mfd^{op} \to Gpd
              sending
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Mfd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\in Mfd
              to the groupoid of trivial principal bundles over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              with
              = succeed "-equivariant" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              = succeed "-valued" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi g
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\xrightarrow{g} G
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi f
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\xrightarrow{f}V
              to the functor induced by pullbacks of principal bundles via
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi BG
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} BG
              back.
